        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "@id": `${canonical}#faq`,
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the best time to visit Morocco?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The best time to visit Morocco is during the spring (mid-March to May) or fall (September to October). The weather is warm but pleasant, unlike the extreme heat of summer."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Do I need a visa to visit Morocco?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Citizens of the US, UK, Canada, Australia, and most of Europe do not need a visa for tourist visits up to 90 days. Always check with your local embassy before traveling."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Is Morocco safe for tourists?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes, Morocco is generally very safe for tourists. The government places a high priority on tourist safety. Like any destination, practice common sense, especially in crowded medinas."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What should female travelers wear in Morocco?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Morocco is a conservative country. While tourists aren't expected to wear traditional clothing, dressing modestly (covering shoulders and knees) is highly recommended out of respect for local culture."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can you drink alcohol in Morocco?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes, alcohol is available in Morocco, primarily in licensed hotels, bars, and tourist restaurants. However, drinking in public outside of these venues is strictly prohibited."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What language do they speak in Morocco?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The official languages are Arabic and Amazigh (Berber). French is widely spoken and serves as the language of commerce. English is increasingly common in tourist areas."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the currency in Morocco?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The currency is the Moroccan Dirham (MAD). While major hotels and large restaurants accept credit cards, cash is essential for souks, taxis, and small shops."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How many days do you need in Morocco?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "To see the highlights (Marrakech, Fes, and the Sahara Desert), we recommend at least 7 to 10 days. A two-week trip allows for a more relaxed pace and exploring the coast."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Is tap water safe to drink in Morocco?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "It is generally recommended for tourists to stick to bottled water to avoid stomach upsets, as the tap water contains different minerals than you might be used to."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
  ]
